WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.140
RAII, Resource Acquisition Is Initialization, is the central C++ pattern for safe cleanup.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:14.520
It ties a resource to an object lifetime so cleanup happens when the owner leaves scope, including during early returns and exceptions.

3
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:21.260
This turns cleanup into a property of the type instead of a set of manual branches that every function must remember.

4
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.740
Standard containers are RAII types for memory.

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:32.200
std::vector, std::string, and std::array manage storage and provide size-aware interfaces.

6
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.300
They reduce the need for raw arrays and manual allocation.

7
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:43.280
Lock guards, file wrappers, and other small resource types apply the same idea to non-memory resources.

8
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:50.820
The rule of zero follows naturally: prefer member types that already manage their resources so your class does not need custom cleanup logic.

9
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.740
Smart pointers express heap ownership.

10
00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.960
std::unique_ptr is usually the clearest default when one owner controls a heap object's lifetime.

11
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.460
Transfer is explicit through moves, and accidental copying is prevented.

12
00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:13.000
std::shared_ptr should mean shared lifetime is real and intentional, not simply that ownership was hard to decide.

13
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:25.500
std::weak_ptr can represent non-owning observation of a shared object, especially where cycles or callbacks would otherwise keep objects alive unexpectedly.

14
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.580
These patterns do not replace design judgment.

15
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:33.480
A smart pointer can still be misused, and a container can still be indexed incorrectly.

16
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.620
The value is that ownership, cleanup, and bounds become easier to review.

17
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:45.680
Prefer standard patterns that make the safe path ordinary and reserve manual allocation or release for narrow, well-documented wrappers.

18
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.640
When a custom wrapper is necessary, keep it small and specific.

19
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:56.940
It should acquire one resource, define whether it can be copied or moved, and release reliably in its destructor.

20
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:03.040
Avoid wrappers that mix cleanup with unrelated policy, logging, or ownership surprises.

21
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:12.020
The more focused the type, the easier it is for ordinary application code to compose safe stack and heap behavior without repeating low-level decisions.
